In [Call Tiering](/work/call-tiering), I designed a way to prioritise outbound sales capacity with intent signals and historical conversion data. Call effort on low-intent leads fell from about 30% to less than 1%. Those leads kept applying on their own, Tier 1 reached its highest application rate on record, and the team did it without adding headcount.

Then the capacity problem came back. A staffing shortfall absorbed the effort we had freed. I asked what would happen when capacity tightened again and three tiers were no longer precise enough.

The Lead Scoring project was designed to answer that question. A decile model scores each lead on its own expected conversion. It was designed and documented, but not launched, so I had no model results to report.

## A cutoff the evidence can support

I ran alignment sessions with six stakeholders across admissions, marketing, paid media and data engineering. I brought their shared requirements to senior leaders, who endorsed the model.

I audited the signals that were actually available and wrote the analysis query myself. It assembled about 600k leads with point-in-time features, meaning each lead's details as they stood at enquiry.

The model had to be interpretable. Each score would surface the context behind it, not just a decile number. Two stakeholders independently raised this need in April 2026.

The dial below shows how a cutoff can move one decile at a time. Its bar heights and positions are illustrative. They do not predict conversion or show a safe amount of call effort to cut.

For each decile, the stopping rule counts only extra applications caused by calls. It uses the application-to-start rate to work out calls per additional student, then prices those calls. The calls stop at the first decile where cost per additional student exceeds the ceiling, lifetime value divided by the target value-to-cost ratio.

Tier 3 gave the team a worked intuition: 290 call attempts per application, while leads applied anyway. That meant very few extra applications came from the calls. Ten deciles let the team draw the stopping line more precisely than three tiers.

## Designed, documented, and handed over

I designed the model, documented it end to end, and handed it to IT unlaunched in August 2026. The work was built to run without me as the dependency.

This builds on [Call Tiering](/work/call-tiering). The model was designed to make the next call decision more precise, but its safe cutoff still needs measured results after launch.
